[[:db/retract 17592186129710 :question/answer-fields 17592186127741] [:db/retract 17592186129581 :question/answer-fields 17592186126969] {:db/id 17592186129305 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-450dd490-a7ea-5123-bd6e-a0e46d7aa855"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128982 :question/problem "Let ![](images/d6/d66afaa2e1560c9fb73e9d839b436717aebdf5ff2a7eafe09f7d3a429eacd94c.svg) and ![](images/c6/c69f65b8989accd6404431ec918e57c3f7853cd198f02507446e08dc8e433f91.svg) be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=-1$ and $\\lambda_2=2,$ respectively.\nWhat is the general solution of the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)?$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129306 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-b90d6d2e-cbeb-5361-979a-20fa4635915b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128952 :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n\n![](images/99/99512ca0bae468975d098cf0789926fe6e8f4ef608693129fa57a3dba02c870b.svg)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-1\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=3.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-1,$ we get \n![](images/45/459df7b451dfbcef264fdf3a1af40f05526529934bb2617a8f43599ee56d27d4.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/b4/b4b518f6ec5abed1cefa88d9de3b42ec18a2553426667def4d7eb4a9964a7e56.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =3,$ we get \n![](images/25/2555ccf5ffe3735fc7cbcd5c5cdbab58b5bf63921ede47ee10ba1192a629b6ba.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/26/26da2e9864ffdb4795701703b58edbee58f73b131cddc7b229b1dc2e007b1678.svg)\n\nThus, the general solution to the given system is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}7 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186128951 :question/worked-solution "Let's construct the matrix differential equation $\\mathbf{x}'(t)=A\\mathbf{x}(t)$ for the given system:\n![](images/b1/b121e46eacd73987eb2d088f4b3904d293e267dd3a005cced0bfd85f55c5b300.svg)\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is lower-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=-3\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\n\\lambda_2=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For $\\lambda_1 =-3,$ we get \n![](images/b9/b97a40ce65d174e23907ba00478f49f633ce1b2bf9fcb5fa3fb7627ed1e27d11.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/a2/a27de99267f5b34b0202c43b984c199264fd2b4808add80c2856bed984ab14f0.svg)\n\n- For $\\lambda_2 =1,$ we get \n![](images/61/61d0d2e59d71dfc71df3bb0604165a54d52fc483196bd452a03a02cb33d0d9e3.svg)\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n![](images/1a/1acdb041f910f83f6cd7d17194e8ce25bc7e82020b55559ff5889b71e052a4c0.svg)\n\nTherefore, we have ![](images/b5/b50817ce9b7984b26e5099dfab0a6205dec82ff90d7069a87901dc92098ec905.svg)\n\nFinally, the change of variables that decouples the system is given by $\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t),$ or equivalently,\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t)=4{u}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)=3{u}_{1}(t)+{u}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-450dd490-a7ea-5123-bd6e-a0e46d7aa855" :question/id #uuid "450dd490-a7ea-5123-bd6e-a0e46d7aa855" :question/math-academy-id "q-79673" :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A$ that correspond to eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ respectively. What is the general solution of the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution of our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fd7ef556-cad9-5361-bc68-0597551b8615"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fd7ef556-cad9-5361-bc68-0597551b8615" :answer-field/id #uuid "fd7ef556-cad9-5361-bc68-0597551b8615" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8fe1f55a-db1f-5973-81e4-e355d50f42c7" "answer-8c5af234-fa4e-5f1f-bbf6-41796e869fad" "answer-271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" "answer-5176a3ef-a7e8-56ee-88b3-c87edbfb88f2" "answer-c17a2582-0132-567b-8489-06c62880c92e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8fe1f55a-db1f-5973-81e4-e355d50f42c7" :answer/id #uuid "8fe1f55a-db1f-5973-81e4-e355d50f42c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c5af234-fa4e-5f1f-bbf6-41796e869fad" :answer/id #uuid "8c5af234-fa4e-5f1f-bbf6-41796e869fad" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" :answer/id #uuid "271feb73-b77f-5623-991f-81df3088d04a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5176a3ef-a7e8-56ee-88b3-c87edbfb88f2" :answer/id #uuid "5176a3ef-a7e8-56ee-88b3-c87edbfb88f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c17a2582-0132-567b-8489-06c62880c92e" :answer/id #uuid "c17a2582-0132-567b-8489-06c62880c92e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b90d6d2e-cbeb-5361-979a-20fa4635915b" :question/id #uuid "b90d6d2e-cbeb-5361-979a-20fa4635915b" :question/math-academy-id "q-327993" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the system of differential equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$*" :question/worked-solution "We need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=3\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=2.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=3,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=2,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d472becc-17da-5ab2-96b6-76c458eb3ce3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d472becc-17da-5ab2-96b6-76c458eb3ce3" :answer-field/id #uuid "d472becc-17da-5ab2-96b6-76c458eb3ce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ec56082-7fc8-5f94-b44a-5cb5efe5d2d0" "answer-8da94f97-391c-5f2d-9aa6-f93861ebaae3" "answer-9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" "answer-b8299918-b9e8-5cbd-b68a-28bb2aaef1f7" "answer-54af6a24-5d99-5408-a766-dca48f108865"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ec56082-7fc8-5f94-b44a-5cb5efe5d2d0" :answer/id #uuid "4ec56082-7fc8-5f94-b44a-5cb5efe5d2d0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8da94f97-391c-5f2d-9aa6-f93861ebaae3" :answer/id #uuid "8da94f97-391c-5f2d-9aa6-f93861ebaae3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" :answer/id #uuid "9e8389b0-350f-5b8b-8932-a02eeebefaa4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b8299918-b9e8-5cbd-b68a-28bb2aaef1f7" :answer/id #uuid "b8299918-b9e8-5cbd-b68a-28bb2aaef1f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54af6a24-5d99-5408-a766-dca48f108865" :answer/id #uuid "54af6a24-5d99-5408-a766-dca48f108865" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "x(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129710 :question/problem "Let ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ be eigenvectors of a matrix $A.$ Find the decoupling matrix of the differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we construct the matrix $E$, whose columns are the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nE=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, the decoupling matrix can be found as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={E}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3\\cdot (-3)-2\\cdot (-4)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-87b3fc9a-3397-5555-bc9f-987194333d19"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-87b3fc9a-3397-5555-bc9f-987194333d19" :answer-field/id #uuid "87b3fc9a-3397-5555-bc9f-987194333d19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-309d74bd-887c-5635-863d-c7f4529c284e" "answer-536f5988-b95f-5a22-8bfe-686e8a9dd306" "answer-04bcc08f-6df3-5da3-a584-9f0a56dba8f6" "answer-054c9cf6-e5e5-5a1d-822d-f6804209660e" "answer-33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-309d74bd-887c-5635-863d-c7f4529c284e" :answer/id #uuid "309d74bd-887c-5635-863d-c7f4529c284e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-536f5988-b95f-5a22-8bfe-686e8a9dd306" :answer/id #uuid "536f5988-b95f-5a22-8bfe-686e8a9dd306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04bcc08f-6df3-5da3-a584-9f0a56dba8f6" :answer/id #uuid "04bcc08f-6df3-5da3-a584-9f0a56dba8f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-054c9cf6-e5e5-5a1d-822d-f6804209660e" :answer/id #uuid "054c9cf6-e5e5-5a1d-822d-f6804209660e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07" :answer/id #uuid "33fd177f-44ac-5b25-b4d6-ccf693b7ba07" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "D=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 2 \\\\ -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130393 :tutorial/content "We know how to solve a system of differential equations when they're decoupled. However, most systems are not decoupled. So, how do we solve them?\n\nLet's consider a general linear system of two differential equations:\n\n![](images/72/72a4d6a819a56d80818184e16205028a3a51eab2e97d1cdad72b8d495e5fd8d1.svg)\n\nIt turns out that if the matrix $A$ has two *distinct real eigenvalues* $\\lambda_1$ and $\\lambda_2$, then $A$ is diagonalizable and we can solve the system.\n\nIf the eigenvectors corresponding to $\\color{red}\\lambda_1$ and $\\color{blue}\\lambda_2$ are ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2},$ respectively, then the general solution to our original system can be written as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}, \\qquad c_1,c_2 \\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nThis result is true for any $n \\times n$ system with $n$ distinct eigenvalues. We'll prove this result for a $2\\times 2$ system at the end of the lesson.\n\nNow, let's see some concrete examples." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130359 :tutorial/content "We've seen how to solve a decoupled system of differential equations. Our goal now is to understand in more detail why this method works. \n\nLet's consider our general linear system of two differential equations once again:\n\n![](images/65/6529692bc6317e2c675e756533a733c0caf087f3ac0b64b9c3a407924014d94b.svg)\n\nWe will show that if the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, we can convert it into an equivalent decoupled system.\n\nIf $A$ is diagonalizable, it has $2$ linearly independent eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$ We use these to form the *change of variables matrix* $E,$ given by\n\n![](images/c1/c11833cbccd43c172ab7699a9000df5e2cf4bfedb0720958f1d5a407e5aeb8f2.svg)\n\nBecause the columns are linearly independent, $E$ is invertible. Then, the substitution  \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = E\\mathbf{u}(t)\n$$\n\nwill transform our original system of differential equations into a decoupled one, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t) & =Ax(t) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(Eu(t)) & =A(Eu(t)) \\\\ E{u}^{′}(t) & =AEu(t) \\\\ {u}^{′}(t) & ={E}^{-1}AE\\,u(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that\n\n![](images/c9/c9657a197b8232d883c6da546170754f2cc6916378d0c269755b3b5aae2173e0.svg)\n\nis a diagonal matrix, and ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1},$ ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2}$ are the eigenvalues of $A$ corresponding to ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}.$\n\nFollowing these steps, we now have the *decoupled system*\n![](images/55/557b8775c900635024b99ba9123d8023b2667db0459b1c8df53df886f4b156f8.svg)\n\nHere, the matrix $D = E^{-1}$ is sometimes called the **decoupling matrix**.\n\nLet's see a concrete example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186130394 :tutorial/content "Consider the $2\\times 2$ matrix differential equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}'(t) = A \\mathbf{x}(t).\n$$\n\n \n\nAssume that $A$ is diagonalizable and has eigenvectors ${\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2}$ with corresponding eigenvalues ${\\color{red}\\lambda_1}$ and ${\\color{blue}\\lambda_2},$ respectively.\n\nWe've been using the general solution formula \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$\n\nBut where does this formula come from? First, remember that the substitution\n\n![](images/7e/7ece1267196b62dfffdf40b16af1b94e9169b6a798f7841f0fd361733042764f.svg)\n\ntransforms the matrix differential equation into the equivalent decoupled system\n\n![](images/a6/a61af1176fb00e767ce5a8fc0ce349b79742937909f70c01701db56b126b010e.svg)\n\nWe can solve this new decoupled system using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the substitution $\\mathbf{x}(t)=E\\mathbf{u}(t)$, we can transform the system back to the variable $\\mathbf x(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =Eu(t) \\\\  & =E({c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}) \\\\  & ={c}_{1}E[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}E[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our original system is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}(t) = c_1{\\color{red}\\mathbf{v}_1} e^{{\\color{red}\\lambda_1} t} + c_2{\\color{blue}\\mathbf{v}_2} e^{{\\color{blue}\\lambda_2} t}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186129398 :activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
